nt pads and rotors, 1.5 hr @ 145.00/hr ... 217.50” | Question this | The quoted front brake work does not match the rear rattle symptom described above. |\n| 5 — “Shop supplies ... 12.50” | Check | Flat fee with no contents stated. |\n| 6 — “Sales tax 7.25% ... 48.29” | Standard | Tax line at a stated rate. |\n| Total — “TOTAL ... 714.29” | Standard | The listed amounts add up to the stated total. |\n\n## Questions to ask\n1. “I described a rattle from the back over bumps. Can you explain how the front brake work and fluid flush relate to that symptom?” — a good answer sounds like: a clear explanation of what was inspected and what finding connects the proposed work to the reported rattle.\n2. “What did you find that indicates the front pads and rotors need replacement?” — a good answer sounds like: a specific inspection finding, explained in plain language.\n3. “What is the brake fluid flush intended to address, and was it identified as part of this symptom or as separate maintenance?” — a good answer sounds like: a clear distinction between work related to the rattle and any separate maintenance.\n4. “What does the shop supplies fee cover?” — a good answer sounds like: a brief explanation of the supplies included in that fee.\n\n## Decision\nThe question script is for understanding the estimate; you decide what to do. Photograph the estimate, note what was promised verbally and by whom, and keep the paperwork chain.\n\n## Needs confirmation\n[CONFIRM: Whether any work has been authorized; the authorization line is blank.]","refusal":null,"reasoning":"**Evaluating documentation practices**\n\nI'm thinking about a neutral documentation habit for anatomy tables that includes all lines, along with totals. I wonder if I should map out parts, labor, fees, and taxes within those tables. Should I clearly label the counts rather than just the total per line category? Also, I might need to check if the total includes anatomy-specific totals. There's the possibility of discr